# Always Commit & Push After Each Edit Prompt

This repo lives inside a OneDrive-synced SharePoint folder. Without explicit
git commits, changes only exist on one machine's local disk and any cross-
machine incident audit is impossible. Azure Automation pulls `reports/`,
`core/`, and `config/` from this SharePoint path at runtime, so knowing which
commit was live when a job ran matters a lot.

## Rules

1. **After every user prompt that results in a completed file edit** (a feature,
   a bug fix, a refactor, CSS/layout tweak, config change, test update, docs
   edit, or rule change), commit to git **before ending your turn**. Do not
   wait to be reminded. Do not batch multiple unrelated tasks into one commit
   -- one logical change, one commit.

2. **Push immediately after committing.** A local commit that hasn't been
   pushed is invisible to the rest of the team and will be lost if the laptop
   dies or OneDrive corrupts the `.git` folder (which happens).

3. **Never `--amend` a pushed commit.** If a follow-up fix is needed after a
   push, make a new commit. The git-safety rules in the system prompt still
   apply.

4. **Write honest commit messages.** Reference the runbook / blueprint /
   report being touched. If the commit fixes a production failure, say so and
   include a brief "why" (not just "fix X"). Keep the subject ≤72 chars.

5. **If `universal_runbook.py` was edited**, also run the Azure publish step
   (`.\deploy-runbook.ps1`) -- see `deploy-runbook.mdc`. Committing alone is
   not enough; Azure Automation keeps running the old runbook until it's
   explicitly republished.

6. **Trivial, in-progress edits don't need their own commit** (e.g. iterating
   on a single function while debugging). But the moment the requested prompt
   is working, verified as much as practical, and ready to hand back, commit
   and push in the same turn.

7. If a user asks "why didn't you push?", treat that as confirmation that this
   workflow applies broadly in this repo. Commit and push the completed work
   immediately unless the user explicitly says to hold, not commit, or not push.

## Workflow

```powershell
git add -- <files>
git commit -m "<concise subject>"
git push
```

If `git push` fails because the remote is ahead, run `git pull --rebase`,
resolve any conflicts, and push again. Never force-push `main` without the
user's explicit, in-conversation confirmation.
